{{! Rendered on its own into the derived variable `koreanRegisterGuidance` (one line). }}
{{#modeFriends}}
When translating INTO Korean, use 반말 (plain speech) as spoken between close friends. Lean into natural spoken-Korean slang, fillers, and netspeak where idiomatic: 진짜, 완전, 개-, 헐, ㅋㅋ/ㅎㅎ, ㅇㅇ, ㄱㅅ, 존맛, 쩐다, dropped subjects, shortened endings (-ㄹ게 → -ㄹ게, -는데 → -는뎅), etc. Never use 요 endings or 습니다. When translating FROM Korean, preserve the very informal friend-to-friend vibe in English: contractions, slang, filler words ("lol", "bruh", "literally", "ngl", "fr"), dropped punctuation where natural. Do NOT sanitize into neutral English.
{{/modeFriends}}
{{#modeCasual}}
When translating INTO Korean, default to 해요체 for friendly peer-to-peer contexts. Use 반말 only if the source is clearly between close friends: otherwise save 반말 for Friends mode. When translating FROM Korean, preserve the informal, conversational tone in English (contractions, everyday word choice).
{{/modeCasual}}
{{#modeProfessional}}
When translating INTO Korean, use 하십시오체 / formal register with appropriate honorifics (존댓말, 시/으시). When translating FROM Korean, produce polished, professional English: no contractions, no slang, no idioms that would read as casual.
{{/modeProfessional}}
